   $q$Why might the author end the fable with Bramble offering to teach Pip instead of turning him away?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To show that careful work can be shared and that Pip still has a chance to change.","is_correct":true},{"text":"To suggest that the moral is wrong and planning ahead is not needed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-theme"},{"text":"To show that Bramble regrets all the time she spent making her map.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"To explain that Bramble has run out of acorns and needs Pip's help.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To show that careful work can be shared and that Pip still has a chance to change.$q$, $q$Bramble gives Pip acorns and offers to teach him "next autumn," so the ending shows her planning helps others and gives Pip a way to plan better. She has plenty of acorns to share, so she is not asking for help.$q$, 'draft', 'c00c3fb3-de69-51d1-b977-2a36f9ee17e4')
